## Requirements

- JDK 25 (Maven comes with the wrapper `./mvnw`)
- Docker, for `docker compose` and the Testcontainers tests
- Access to the GitHub Packages repository that publishes axiz

## Build and test

```bash
./mvnw verify                 # enforcer rules, tests and architecture rules
./mvnw spotless:apply         # formats the code (run it before pushing)
./mvnw -Pquality verify       # CI gate: + format check, Checkstyle, Error Prone + NullAway, SpotBugs/FindSecBugs,
                              # dependency convergence and coverage thresholds
./mvnw -Pnative native:compile  # GraalVM native image
```

`ArchitectureTest` enforces the rules of the `{{fw.id}}` style and the general axiz rules;
`{{project.className}}ApplicationTest` starts the service against real dependencies in containers.
`SensitiveDataCanaryTest` uses synthetic PAN, CVV and password values to check the configured
logging, audit sanitization, error mapping and server response converter/encoder. It covers
success, failure, timeout and rollback evidence; JPA services additionally trigger a real
PostgreSQL constraint failure and rollback. Extend it with your DTOs and custom integrations.
Never run a canary with real sensitive values.
{{#hasExamples}}
`ContractExamplesTest` checks that every request example of the contract is a valid request.
{{/hasExamples}}

{{#auditResources.required}}
Audit tests create their own LocalStack resources before auto-configuration and register the returned URL/ARN.
The framework creates the application clients; the test context owns and closes its container. They do not
require `AXIZ_AUDIT_DESTINATION` or `AXIZ_AUDIT_QUEUE_URL` in the environment. The local profile
uses the `{{project.name}}-audit` resource created by Compose init. Production must supply its
configured audit destination; the local values are not production defaults.
{{/auditResources.required}}

